# Warrant binding table (VER04i section 5.1) for code/specs/ct-compare.warrant.
#
# One line per Warrant operation: which public export implements it and how a
# raised exception becomes a declared `Err` variant.  This file is data, not
# code -- it cannot compute an answer, retry or fall back; the runner
# (`coding_adventures_warrant_corpus`) reads nothing else about this package.
#
# `width=implicit`: `ct_eq_fixed` infers the width N from its buffers; the
# runner asserts every Bytes<N> argument really has length N and does not pass N.
#
# The `errors=` row maps by EXACT exception class, not message: a subclass of
# ValueError is not the declared error.  But `ct_select_bytes` raises a plain
# ValueError for the length mismatch and `_as_bytes` raises plain ValueError for
# bad elements, so any other plain ValueError would still read as the
# length-mismatch error.  The corpus only passes `bytes`, so that cannot happen
# today; a dedicated exception type would close it (backlog, portable Warrant
# queue).  That remaining limit is VER04i section 11, item 3.
lane python
package python/ct-compare
module ct_compare
import coding_adventures_ct_compare
source src/coding_adventures_ct_compare
seed 0x5741_5252_414e_5421
values Bool=bool Bytes=bytes Nat=int U64=int

op eq export=ct_eq
op eq_fixed export=ct_eq_fixed width=implicit
op select_bytes export=ct_select_bytes errors=CT_SELECT_LENGTH_MISMATCH:ValueError
op eq_u64 export=ct_eq_u64
